[
    {
        "content": "<p>When I tried looking for code formalising (linear) algebraic groups, the closest thing I could find were algebraic motives. Hence my question:<br>\nAre (linear) algebraic groups, or some basic definitions and theorems, already in Mathlib, and if not, in another repository?</p>",
        "id": 595666343,
        "sender_full_name": "Django Peeters",
        "timestamp": 1779053366
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>What the heck is an algebraic motive? is this some LLM hallucinating or you misunderstanding what a computer scientist thinks a motive is? (it's the thing you're proving by induction, it has nothing to do with Grothendieck motives). </p>\n<p>Here's a proposed definition of linear algebraic group over a field:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">import</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Mathlib</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">open</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">CategoryTheory</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">AlgebraicGeometry</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">open</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">scoped</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">SpecOfNotation</span>\n\n<span class=\"c1\">-- let K be a field</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">variable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">K</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Field</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">K</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n\n<span class=\"c1\">-- let G be an affine group scheme of finite type over K</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">variable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">G</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Scheme</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">φ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">G</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⟶</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Spec</span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">K</span><span class=\"o\">))</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">GrpObj</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Over</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">mk</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">φ</span><span class=\"o\">)]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">IsAffine</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">G</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">LocallyOfFiniteType</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">φ</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>I don't think this is in mathlib but my understanding is that this is what the experts think should be going in mathlib.</p>",
        "id": 595670228,
        "sender_full_name": "Kevin Buzzard",
        "timestamp": 1779056675
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Maybe I misremembered schemes from algebraic geometry...</p>",
        "id": 595670471,
        "sender_full_name": "Django Peeters",
        "timestamp": 1779056906
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Thank you for the definition!</p>",
        "id": 595670592,
        "sender_full_name": "Django Peeters",
        "timestamp": 1779056995
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Schemes are certainly in mathlib. Just to be clear -- motives in the sense of Grothendieck certainly aren't.</p>",
        "id": 595671114,
        "sender_full_name": "Kevin Buzzard",
        "timestamp": 1779057441
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Some authors also include conditions like reduced and connected.</p>",
        "id": 595706477,
        "sender_full_name": "Scott Carnahan",
        "timestamp": 1779075782
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Right, but I think we're in one of those situations where we will not end up with <code>LinearAlgebraicGroup</code>, we will just spell out what we need, like how we won't end up with a definition of <code>Variety</code> because it means different things to different people. I will certainly want <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu_p</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7167em;vertical-align:-0.2861em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">μ</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.1514em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:0em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">p</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.2861em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> (not connected in characteristic zero, not reduced in characteristic <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.625em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">p</span></span></span></span>) as an algebraic group for FLT. </p>\n<p>I think the definition of linear algebraic group above can't just go into mathlib because it's just something we can \"already say\" (the way I said it). I think the real question right now is whether we can go backwards and forwards between affine algebraic groups over a general ring base and Hopf algebras. I think these are some approximation to the constructions we need in order to get started.</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">import</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Mathlib</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">open</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">CategoryTheory</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">AlgebraicGeometry</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">CommRingCat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Scheme</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Opposite</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Spec</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">open</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">scoped</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">SpecOfNotation</span>\n\n<span class=\"c1\">-- let R be a commutative ring</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">variable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">u</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">CommRing</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n\n<span class=\"sd\">/-- The R-Hopf algebra structure on Γ(G) for G an affine group scheme over</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">Spec(R). -/</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">G</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Scheme</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">φ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">G</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⟶</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Spec</span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"o\">))</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">GrpObj</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Over</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">mk</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">φ</span><span class=\"o\">)]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">IsAffine</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">G</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">HopfAlgebra</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Γ</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">obj</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">op</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">G</span><span class=\"o\">))</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"gr\">sorry</span>\n\n<span class=\"c\">/-</span>\n<span class=\"cm\">The affine group scheme on Spec(A) over Spec(R) associated to a Hopf algebra A over R</span>\n<span class=\"cm\">-/</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">A</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">u</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">CommRing</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">A</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">HopfAlgebra</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">A</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">GrpObj</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Over</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">mk</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">map</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">ofHom</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">algebraMap</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">A</span><span class=\"o\">))</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Spec</span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">A</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⟶</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Spec</span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"o\">)))</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"gr\">sorry</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p><span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"387244\">@Yaël Dillies</span> are we close to either of these? I think the last time I asked you said we were waiting on stuff to be upstreamed from Toric. Have I got these formalizations right?</p>",
        "id": 595759123,
        "sender_full_name": "Kevin Buzzard",
        "timestamp": 1779095353
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>The second one we have in Toric (please review <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/39281\">#39281</a>,! this is the current PR we are waiting on). I believe we have the first one too but I'll need to check</p>",
        "id": 595787272,
        "sender_full_name": "Yaël Dillies",
        "timestamp": 1779102389
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"110038\">Kevin Buzzard</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/217875-Is-there-code-for-X.3F/topic/Algebraic.20groups/near/595759123\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>the real question right now is whether we can go backwards and forwards between affine algebraic groups over a general ring base and Hopf algebras.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Yes, we can. This is done in Toric</p>",
        "id": 595787586,
        "sender_full_name": "Yaël Dillies",
        "timestamp": 1779102462
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I am somewhat annoyed by the upstreaming situation of Toric: The code has been ready for a year, there isn't much of it, but the upstreaming has to be done mostly linearly and every PR is ignored for two months then quickly merged with only minor changes. Can I motivate someone (Kevin?) to look at the upstreaming PRs more closely and ensure they don't wait forever?</p>",
        "id": 595788075,
        "sender_full_name": "Yaël Dillies",
        "timestamp": 1779102584
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>This is reaching the point where people (including Kim) duplicate our efforts because they see that we are too slow at upstreaming/aren't aware of what's in Toric <span aria-label=\"sad\" class=\"emoji emoji-2639\" role=\"img\" title=\"sad\">:sad:</span></p>",
        "id": 595788463,
        "sender_full_name": "Yaël Dillies",
        "timestamp": 1779102679
    },
    {
